 ## Overview
-Triton-accelerated Volume Raycasting is a fused GPU rendering technique that uses the Triton programming language to perform volume raycasting, avoiding the materialization of intermediate ray-sample tensors in High Bandwidth Memory (HBM) to reduce memory footprint and increase speed [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard PyTorch implementations by fusing CUDA kernels, which reduces iteration time and memory overhead, particularly at high resolutions [S1, unofficial]. While standard PyTorch execution degrades super-linearly beyond 1024x124 resolution due to memory bandwidth limits and cache thrashing, Triton maintains predictable linear scaling proportional to the total number of rays rendered [S1, unofficial].
+Triton-accelerated Volume Raycasting is a fused GPU rendering technique using the Triton programming language to perform volume raycasting, avoiding the materialization of intermediate ray-sample tensors in High Bandwidth Memory (HBM) to reduce memory footprint and increase speed [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard PyTorch implementations by fusing CUDA kernels, which reduces iteration time and memory overhead, particularly at high resolutions [S1, unofficial]. While standard PyTorch execution degrades super-linearly beyond 1024x1024 resolution due to memory bandwidth limits and cache thrashing, Triton maintains predictable linear scaling proportional to the total number of rays rendered [S1, unofficial].
 
